WEBVTT

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:01.939
An AI report says

00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:04.466
this property earns 8% a year.

00:00:04.606 --> 00:00:05.644
So why are you putting

00:00:05.644 --> 00:00:07.281
money into it every month?

00:00:08.471 --> 00:00:11.627
Imagine a home costs $300,000.

00:00:11.627 --> 00:00:13.865
It could rent
for $2,000 a month.

00:00:14.455 --> 00:00:17.502
Twelve months gives $24,000.

00:00:17.892 --> 00:00:19.302
Divide that by the price,

00:00:19.302 --> 00:00:21.031
and you get 8%.

00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:24.121
That’s called
gross rental yield:

00:00:24.121 --> 00:00:26.310
rent compared with
the property’s price,

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:27.388
before costs.

00:00:27.388 --> 00:00:29.340
It is not money
you get to keep.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.467
Now, one month passes
without a tenant.

00:00:33.957 --> 00:00:35.197
$2,000 disappears

00:00:35.197 --> 00:00:37.635
from the expected rent.

00:00:38.925 --> 00:00:42.301
Property taxes, insurance,
repairs and management

00:00:42.301 --> 00:00:45.053
cost another $7,000
over the year.

00:00:45.643 --> 00:00:47.670
You have $15,000 left.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.819
But your loan payments
are $18,000.

00:00:52.209 --> 00:00:54.719
You need $3,000
from your own pocket.

00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:57.945
That’s $250 a month,
on average.

00:01:00.135 --> 00:01:02.485
Some loan payments
repay what you borrowed,

00:01:02.485 --> 00:01:04.053
building your ownership.

00:01:04.053 --> 00:01:05.663
So this is a cash shortfall,

00:01:05.663 --> 00:01:08.456
not the whole investment’s
profit or loss.

00:01:09.846 --> 00:01:12.357
The 8% calculation
wasn’t wrong.

00:01:12.357 --> 00:01:15.110
It just answered a smaller
question than you thought.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.130
AI can help analyse the deal.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.570
But check whether
its rent is realistic,

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.150
which costs it includes,

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.789
and what happens
when the home sits empty.

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:28.313
Before trusting the headline,
ask: what comes in,

00:01:28.313 --> 00:01:29.269
what goes out,

00:01:29.269 --> 00:01:31.233
and what’s left
after the loan?

00:01:32.723 --> 00:01:35.118
8% of what,
before which costs?

